---
/**
 * Twenty minutes of writing that states the entire differentiator plainly.
 *
 * It's also the signal that gets a small site cited by an AI rather than
 * retrieved and discarded — models weight sourcing and provenance, and this
 * page is the provenance statement. Linked from the footer of every page.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: "How we verify",
    description:
      "Every fact on Delaware Field Guide carries the date it was checked and the source it came from. Here is the method, and what happens when we get something wrong.",
    mainEntityOfPage: new URL("/how-we-verify/", Astro.site).href,
    author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Delaware Field Guide" },
    publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Delaware Field Guide" },
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Where do the rules on this site come from?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "From the body that sets them — the city, the state park, or the property itself — by phone or from its own published notice. Never from another guide.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "What does the verification date mean?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "It is the date we last confirmed that fact with its source. It is not the date the page was published or edited. If a page has no verification stamp, it has not been confirmed and it is not indexed.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "What happens when a fact turns out to be wrong?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "We fix it, re-date it, and say on the page that it was corrected and when. Corrections are not quietly edited away.",
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="How we verify — Delaware Field Guide"
  description="Every fact here carries the date it was checked and the source it came from. The method, in full, including what happens when we get something wrong."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap pt-10 pb-2">
    <p class="label m-0">Method</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.15rem] sm:text-[2.6rem] mt-2 mb-0">How we verify</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <p class="text-[1.2rem] leading-[1.5] font-medium max-w-[58ch]">
      We call the source, we date every fact, and we publish corrections. That's
      the whole method. It is unglamorous and it is the entire reason this site
      exists.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this page exists at all</h2>
    <p>
      Most guides to the Delaware beaches copy their dog rules from each other.
      That works until a town changes a rule — and then the error propagates
      across a dozen sites and sits there for years, because nobody re-checked
      and nobody had a source to re-check against.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is not hypothetical. Rehoboth Beach moved its seasonal dog dates in
      January 2026. A large share of the pages you'll find still publish the
      old ones. Being right about that is not a content-marketing flourish; it
      is the difference between a wasted three-hour drive and a good weekend.
    </p>

    <h2>The rules</h2>

    <h3>1. The source is the body that sets the rule</h3>
    <p>
      A town's dog rules come from the town — by phone, or from the town's own
      published notice. A state park rule comes from the park. A pet fee comes
      from the property, not from a booking site's summary of the property. We
      do not source a fact from another guide, ever, including when that guide
      is probably right.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Every fact carries the date it was checked</h3>
    <p>
      Not the date the page went up, and not the date we last touched the file
      — the date we last confirmed the fact with its source. That's what the
      mono stamp at the bottom of each page is. It tells you how much to trust
      the page, which is information you can't get from a page that doesn't
      have one.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Unverified means unpublished</h3>
    <p>
      A page whose facts we haven't confirmed doesn't get a stamp, doesn't get
      indexed, and doesn't appear in the section list. This is enforced by the
      site's build, not by our good intentions: pages with no verification date
      and no named source are automatically marked <code class="stamp">noindex</code>
      and dropped from the sitemap. We can't accidentally ship a draft as fact.
    </p>

    <h3>4. "Not confirmed" is a real answer</h3>
    <p>
      Where we don't know something, the page says "not confirmed" rather than
      guessing or quietly omitting it. An empty field on a park listing means we
      haven't checked it — it does not mean the park lacks the thing.
    </p>

    <h3 id="corrections">5. Corrections are published, not buried</h3>
    <p>
      When we get something wrong, we fix it, re-date it, and note on the page
      that it was corrected and when. A site that claims to be verified and
      silently rewrites its mistakes is worth less than one that never claimed
      it.
    </p>

    <h2>What we don't do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        We don't run a live water-quality or bloom tracker. Conditions change
        hourly and a stale "safe today" badge is worse than no badge. We link to
        DNREC and the Division of Public Health, who actually monitor it.
      </li>
      <li>
        We don't publish a rule we've only seen on an aggregator, no matter how
        many aggregators agree. Agreement between copies is not corroboration.
      </li>
      <li>
        We don't take payment to change or soften a listing. Lodging links may
        earn a commission; the fees, weight limits and policies we publish are
        what the property told us, and an unfavourable answer gets published the
        same as a favourable one.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Found something wrong?</h2>
    <p>
      Tell us and we'll check it and correct it. Local regulars know these rules
      better than any publisher does, and a correction from someone who was
      standing there yesterday is worth more than another phone call.
      <a href="https://werth.design" rel="noopener">Get in touch through Werth Design</a>,
      who build and maintain this site.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
